  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday night through Saturday)\nIssued at 248 AM EST Sun Nov 1 2026\n\nAs the long term period picks up on Monday night, an amplified upper \nlevel pattern will be in place over much of the CONUS, with a ridge \naxis extending from the northeast Gulf through Georgia and towards \nthe northern Great Lakes and a strong trough swinging through the \ncentral Great Plains. Surface high pressure associated with the \nridge will be centered near the Mid-Atlantic coast, keeping a \nshallow wedge in place across north and east portions of the \nforecast area. Low temperatures on Tuesday morning will begin in the \nmid 50s in north Georgia and upper 50s to low 60s in central \nGeorgia. Breezy easterly winds in the wedge pattern should inhibit \nwidespread fog formation in spite of increasing moisture. The upper \nlevel pattern will transition eastward over the course of the day on \nTuesday, with Georgia becoming positioned underneath southwesterly \nflow aloft. As this flow strengthens, deep moisture will surge into \nthe region and dewpoints will rise into the 60s while precipitable \nwater values rise to between 1.75-2 inches.\n\nUncertainty remains with respect to the trough, its evolution, and \nhow it interacts with the lingering wedge as it approaches the \nSoutheast by midweek. A surface low under the trough will extend a \ncold front southward, which will move from west to east towards the \narea. Forcing ahead of the front and moist, relatively unstable air \nwill support widespread rain and numerous thunderstorms across \nthe forecast area. Rainfall amounts are forecast to range from 1-2\ninches in areas along and north of I-85, with lower amounts to \nthe south. Chances are currently expected to rise on Wednesday \nmorning and continue through the day on Thursday, though the \ntiming, rainfall amounts, and severe weather potential will depend\non the evolution of the trough and associated low pressure \nsystem. Low temperatures during the middle to later parts of the \nweek will closely reflect dewpoints, while high temperatures are \nforecast to be mainly in the low to mid 70s in north Georgia and \nupper 70s to low 80s in central Georgia.",
